Dear all,

I want to keep you informed about the IPQ6018 debugging I'm doing.
Situation looks as though raw mode is broken in all the firmware's I've
tested so far (2.4.0.1/2.5.0.1/2.6.0.1).

Note that this is only my opinion and merely based on the observations
explained below.


I did the following in between: set-up an AP on the WiFi (channel 48,
5GHz) with frame_mode=3D1 and ran iperf3 receiving TCP:
[ ID] Interval Transfer Bitrate Retr [ 5] 0.00-60.06 sec 4.15 GBytes 594 Mb=
its/sec 1 sender [ 5] 0.00-60.00 sec 4.15 GBytes 594 Mbits/sec receiver

Good data rate.

Switched to raw mode (frame_mode=3D0), leaving everything else as-is and
observed:

[ ID] Interval Transfer Bitrate Retr
[ 5] 0.00-10.05 sec 1.28 MBytes 1.07 Mbits/sec 225 sender
[ 5] 0.00-10.00 sec 840 KBytes 688 Kbits/sec receiver

Received bitrate is really *BAD*, I even had to reduce the capture time
because the kernel kept rebooting the device due to "skb_over_panic".


Tests with UDP look better (much higher rates but frame drops between
20% and 40%. This naturally explains why TCP rates are so bad: the
frame drops cause the TCP stream to stall frequently, waiting for
retransmissions and make the rate control algo go to the bottom.


This excludes 802.11s mesh implementation at all and points to the raw
mode in special (which appears to be required for mesh support).
Furthermore it allows to conclude that there's likely nothing wrong
with the patches applied to the kernel (mac80211, ath11k, device-
specific clocks/gpios/resets, DFS and the like) because that would mean
that non-raw mode is broken as well.
I'm making use of the fact here almost all the errors are triggered
directly after getting the DMA buffers from the hardware and even
before raw mode is distinguished from ieee80211 frame mode.



Looking at the error messages the kernel spews, most of them regard
earliest sanity checks on the DMA buffers:

ath11k c000000.wifi: msdu len not valid
- function ath11k_dp_rx_process_msdu() in dp_rx.c
- struct hal_rx_desc at start of DMA buffer is completely invalid

ath11k c000000.wifi: invalid msdu len 12532
- function ath11k_dp_rx_process_msdu() in dp_rx.c
- struct hal_rx_desc.rx_msdu_start_ipq8074 is completely invalid

Other messages such as
 * Rate marked as an HT rate but passed status->rate_idx is not an MCS
   index [0-76]: 248 (0xf8)
 * WARNING: CPU: 3 PID: 354 at net/mac80211/rx.c:5293
   ieee80211_rx_list+0x510/0xba4 [mac80211]
appear more seldom and relate to just specific parts in the hal_rx_desc
structure to be invalid.


I've added a kernel logfile with quite some DMA buffer data dumps of
packets rejected by ath11k_dp_rx_process_msdu().

In cases of "msdu len not valid" it is seen that the hal_rx_desc
contains completely invalid data (that's not constant over time) but
always appears to begin with the same byte sequence 0x80 0x02 0x00 0x00
0x9e 0x81 0x01. (I had to add the debug dump to the driver to see the
descriptor)

In cases of "invalid msdu len %d" it is seen that the first part of the
hal_rx_desc containing rx_msdu_end_ipq8074 as well as rx_attention are
still OK, but directly afterwards the buffer becomes invalid from
msdu_start_tag on (lines 377, 1182 in the logfile, corresponding error
code is EINVAL [-22] instead of EIO [-5]). The extracted ieee80211_hdr
that's printed before hal_rx_desc is completely invalid as well and has
nothing to do with the expected data.

Observing that:
   1. everything is working well in ieee80211 frame mode, and
   2. the hardware queue fetch code and subsequent frame analysis in
      ath11k_dp_rx_process_msdu() is the same for each frame mode,
it appears the the kernel and ath11k driver themselfs do not suffer
from cache coherency issues, timing/clocking issues or HW queue
management errors.

That's why I think there's still a firmware bug in frame_mode=3D0 that
triggers "occasionally" (less than half of the frames) in higher load
scenarios.

I would love to get mesh working without raw mode, but I can't. The
symptom is that regular non-mgmt frames are not delivered by the
firmware to the regular queues, even though the packets are visible on
a monitor interface created on the same ATH11k phy instance.

Kind regards,
Stefan


